   221	        read's lens and the conformance vectors (SPEC §9): ``ts`` and ``id``
   222	        resolve against the record **envelope**, every other key against the
   223	        flat **payload**. One declaration, one projection, whatever surface
   224	        asks. A payload field named ``ts`` or ``id`` does NOT shadow the
   225	        envelope — that is what keeps ``ByKey('ts')`` from meaning two
   226	        different things depending on what a payload happens to carry.
   227	        ``ts`` being a stored column is an optimization detail here, not a
   228	        change of semantics; this surface hands it over as a ``datetime``,
   229	        which orders identically to the raw REAL and stays single-typed.
   230	
   231	        The other stored columns — ``kind``, ``observer``, ``origin`` — are
   232	        NOT key candidates on any surface: ``ByKey('kind')`` is a payload key
   233	        that almost no fact carries, and the projection is empty by
   234	        non-membership rather than grouping the stream by its column.
   235	
   236	        A fact that lacks ``K`` where ``K`` resolves is not in the
   237	        ``ByKey(K)`` projection at all — a key names what it projects and
   238	        absence is non-membership, so the returned list can be shorter than
   239	        ``prefix``.
