    10	reason: ``SqliteStore.append``/``append_tick`` do the mint work and then
    11	persist one fully-assembled row through the ``_write_fact_row`` /
    12	``_write_tick_row`` / ``_ceremony_persist`` seam. Overriding the seam sees
    13	the committed row for free and inherits every read method unchanged.
    14	
    15	How a row becomes a record
    16	--------------------------
    17	The arrival record's ``body`` is the line codec's object for the committed
    18	row, verbatim — ``payload`` rides as the stored TEXT string inside it, so
    19	every existing fact signature and commitment hash survives the round trip,
    20	and a later cut can rebuild the index by handing ``body`` straight back to
    21	the codec. No second encoding exists to disagree with. The record's ``k``
    22	is the row class (``fact`` / ``tick`` / ``batch``), never the fact's own
    23	kind — a fact kind is data and rides inside ``body``, where it cannot
    24	collide with the grammar's structural kinds (genesis, key introductions).
    25	``observer``/``origin``/``at`` mirror the row's authorship columns (a tick
    26	has no observer; its record carries the tick's name there, which is the
    27	authorship a tick has).
    28	
    29	Fact records are signed by the store's injected ``fact_signer`` over the
    30	ARRIVAL content commitment — record-level attestation, resolvable by
    31	:func:`engine.arrival.verify_authorship` from log coordinates alone. The
    32	row's own signature (over the fact commitment) rides inside ``body`` as
    33	data for the untouched legacy verify path; the two claims are different
    34	subjects and both survive.
    35	
    36	Write order per append: reconcile, stage the sqlite INSERT (uncommitted),
    37	read the committed row back, append the record to the arrival log (durable,
    38	fsync'd, under the log's own lock), stamp the resume mark, commit. A
    39	failure after the log fsync rolls the index back and leaves the record
    40	durable and unindexed — the recoverable state catch-up consumes.
    41	
    42	Catch-up: consume, refuse — never rebuild
    43	------------------------------------------
    44	The index's cursor is the ratified three-field resume mark
    45	(``arrival_lineage`` / ``arrival_offset`` / ``arrival_ordinal`` in
    46	``store_meta``), validated at adoption by :meth:`engine.arrival.ArrivalLog.
    47	walk_marked` — the anchor is re-checked at the site that trusts it, never
    48	assumed. A fresh, empty index builds forward from ordinal 0 (building an
    49	absent projection destroys nothing). Everything else that cannot be
    50	consumed forward REFUSES with :class:`ArrivalCanonicalUnsupported`:
    51	discarding rows and re-deriving the index is projection re-derivation, the
    52	next cut's verb, and giving this class a repair verb now would pre-empt
    53	that design (the same reasoning that keeps :meth:`adopt_lineage` a
    54	refusal).
    55	
    56	Detection scope, stated narrowly: the mark is offset-and-ordinal parity
    57	plus the anchor checks. An out-of-band sqlite INSERT that leaves the mark
    58	intact opens clean here — the arrival-side agreement audit is a later
    59	cut's, alongside re-derivation. The log itself stays the authority either
    60	way: everything the gate asks answers from the log alone.
    61	"""
